{% extends "@core/docs/_layout.twig" %}

{% block doc_content %}
    <h1>The Path Helper</h1>
    <p>
        <code>Rhapsody\Core\Helpers\Path</code> is a small static helper that returns <strong>absolute filesystem paths</strong> to the important directories of your application. Whenever your code needs to read, write, include, or check a file, build the location with <code>Path</code> instead of hand-written relative paths. It is dependency-free, requires no setup, and costs next to nothing at runtime.
    </p>

    <pre><code class="language-php">use Rhapsody\Core\Helpers\Path;

$logFile = Path::logs('import.log');
// /var/www/mysite/storage/logs/import.log</code></pre>

    <div class="bg-blue-100 border-l-4 border-blue-500 text-blue-700 p-4">
        <strong>Paths, not URLs:</strong> <code>Path</code> gives you locations on the <em>server's disk</em>. It never returns a web address. See <a href="#paths-vs-urls">Paths vs URLs</a> below.
    </div>

    <hr>

    <h2>Why use it?</h2>
    <p>Hard-coded and relative paths are one of the most common sources of &ldquo;works on my machine&rdquo; bugs. <code>Path</code> removes the whole category:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Independent of the working directory.</strong> A relative path like <code>'storage/logs/app.log'</code> is resolved against the <em>current working directory</em>, which is not the same for a web request, a CLI command, a cron job, or a persistent worker. <code>Path</code> always starts from your project root.</li>
        <li><strong>No <code>../../../</code> chains.</strong> Moving a file to another folder never silently breaks the paths inside it.</li>
        <li><strong>Cross-platform.</strong> Slashes in the path you pass in are normalised to the operating system's native separator, so the same code works on Windows (WAMP), Linux, and macOS.</li>
        <li><strong>One source of truth.</strong> If a directory ever moves, there is one place to update, not dozens of string literals.</li>
    </ul>

    <hr>

    <h2>Available Methods</h2>
    <p>Every method accepts an optional <code>$path</code> string that is appended to the directory.</p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-full text-left border border-gray-300 dark:border-gray-700">
            <thead class="bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800">
                <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-300 dark:border-gray-700">Method</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-300 dark:border-gray-700">Resolves to</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>Path::root()</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">The application root (the folder that contains <code>vendor/</code>)</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>Path::app()</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>app/</code> – your controllers, models, and other application code</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>Path::config()</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>config/</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>Path::public()</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>public/</code> – used to store CSS / JS and images</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>Path::storage()</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>storage/</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>Path::cache()</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>storage/cache/</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>Path::logs()</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>storage/logs/</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>Path::resources()</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>resources/</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>Path::views()</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>views/</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2"><code>Path::database()</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2"><code>db/</code> – migrations, seeds, and similar files (note the folder is named <code>db</code>, not <code>database</code>)</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Appending a sub-path</h3>
    <p>Pass the rest of the location as the argument. You can use forward slashes everywhere, even on Windows:</p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">Path::app('Controllers/HomeController.php');
Path::config('mail.php');
Path::public('uploads/avatars');
Path::views('emails/welcome.twig');
Path::root('.env');</code></pre>

    <h3>What you can rely on</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Results <strong>never end with a separator</strong>, with or without a sub-path. Pass the entire sub-path as the argument rather than concatenating pieces yourself.</li>
        <li>Forward and backward slashes in the argument are converted to the OS's native separator.</li>
        <li>Leading slashes in the argument are stripped, so <code>Path::config('/mail.php')</code> and <code>Path::config('mail.php')</code> are identical. A leading slash does <strong>not</strong> mean &ldquo;the root of the server's disk&rdquo;.</li>
        <li><code>Path</code> only builds strings. It does <strong>not</strong> check that the file exists and does <strong>not</strong> create missing folders.</li>
    </ul>

    <hr>

    <h2>Common Examples</h2>

    <h3>Reading a config file</h3>
    <pre><code class="language-php">$settings = require Path::config('custom-settings.php');</code></pre>

    <h3>Writing to a custom log file</h3>
    <pre><code class="language-php">$file = Path::logs('payments.log');

file_put_contents($file, date('c') . " Payment received\n", FILE_APPEND | LOCK_EX);</code></pre>

    <h3>Saving an uploaded file</h3>
    <pre><code class="language-php">$directory = Path::public('uploads/avatars');

if (!is_dir($directory)) {
    mkdir($directory, 0755, true);
}

move_uploaded_file($tmpName, $directory . DIRECTORY_SEPARATOR . $safeFilename);</code></pre>
    <p>For a complete upload workflow with validation, see the <a href="{{ base_url }}/docs/file-uploader">File Uploader</a> documentation.</p>

    <h3>Pointing at a theme's assets</h3>
    <pre><code class="language-php">$theme = $_ENV['APP_THEME'] ?? 'default';

$imageDirectory = Path::public('themes/' . $theme . '/images');</code></pre>
    <p>This is also how you would change where the image processor looks for source images. See <a href="{{ base_url }}/docs/image-processing">Image Processing</a>.</p>

    <h3>Checking for a file</h3>
    <pre><code class="language-php">if (is_file(Path::storage('exports/report.csv'))) {
    // ...
}</code></pre>

    <hr>

    <h2>How the Project Root Is Found</h2>
    <p>Every other method is built on <code>Path::root()</code>, which determines the base directory like this:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>If a <code>ROOT_DIR</code> constant is defined, it is used. The application's <code>public/index.php</code> defines this for you.</li>
        <li>Otherwise <code>Path</code> falls back to walking up from its own location inside <code>vendor/arout/rhapsody-core/src/Helpers/</code> to the project root.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>With a standard install you never need to think about this. If you use a non-standard directory layout, define <code>ROOT_DIR</code> in your entry point before the framework boots and every <code>Path</code> method will follow it.</p>
    <p>Because the fallback relies on the package living inside <code>vendor/</code>, make sure <code>ROOT_DIR</code> is defined in any custom entry script that runs from somewhere unusual (for example a standalone CLI or worker script).</p>

    <hr>

    <h2 id="paths-vs-urls">Paths vs URLs</h2>
    <p>This is the most common point of confusion. <code>Path</code> answers &ldquo;where is this file on the server?&rdquo;. A browser needs to be told &ldquo;what address do I request?&rdquo;.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">// Filesystem path – use for PHP file operations
Path::public('uploads/photo.jpg');
// C:\wamp64\www\mysite\public\uploads\photo.jpg   (Windows)
// /var/www/mysite/public/uploads/photo.jpg        (Linux)</code></pre>
    <pre><code class="language-twig">{# Web address – use in templates and links #}
&lt;img src="{{ '{{ base_url }}' }}/uploads/photo.jpg"&gt;</code></pre>
    <p>Rule of thumb: <strong>PHP file functions get a <code>Path</code>; HTML gets a URL.</strong> Never output a <code>Path</code> value into a page, as it would expose your server's directory structure.</p>

    <hr>

    <h2>Security: Never Pass Raw User Input</h2>
    <p><code>Path</code> is a path builder, not a sandbox. It does not strip <code>../</code> segments, so a value taken straight from a request could escape the folder you intended.</p>
    <div class="bg-red-200 border-l-4 border-red-700 text-red-700 p-4">
        <strong>Danger:</strong> <code>Path::storage('uploads/' . $_GET['file'])</code> can be abused with <code>?file=../../.env</code>.
    </div>
    <p>Reduce user input to a bare filename with <code>basename()</code>, or verify the resolved location stays inside the expected folder:</p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">$base = realpath(Path::storage('uploads'));
$file = realpath(Path::storage('uploads/' . $name));

if ($base === false || $file === false || !str_starts_with($file, $base . DIRECTORY_SEPARATOR)) {
    // Reject the request
}</code></pre>

    <hr>

    <h2>Adding Your Own Locations</h2>
    <p>Need a shortcut for a folder your app uses constantly? Extend <code>Path</code> in your application and add a method. All of the inherited methods keep working.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">namespace App\Helpers;

use Rhapsody\Core\Helpers\Path;

class AppPath extends Path
{
    public static function uploads(string $path = ''): string
    {
        return self::storage('uploads/' . ltrim($path, '/\\'));
    }
}

// Usage
AppPath::uploads('invoices/2026-001.pdf');</code></pre>
    <p>Remember the Golden Rule: never edit files in <code>/vendor</code>. Extending the class in your own <code>app/</code> directory keeps your additions safe during framework updates.</p>

